    "body": "Accepted truckload volume gave back its recent gains and fell to the 13th percentile, intermodal contract rates dropped to the 65th percentile and ocean bookings declined for a second straight week. Rail volume held near its five-year high, the national spot rate firmed to $3.56 and rejections eased to their lowest level since early May.\nThis week’s SONAR Pricing Power Index (PPI): 68 (down from 72).\nThe composite fell four points, with most of the decline coming from demand. Accepted truckload volume fell about 4% to 9,598, dropping to the 13th percentile from 27th, and intermodal contract rates fell to $1.70 a mile, slipping to the 65th percentile from 78th. Ocean bookings eased to the 43rd percentile from 48th, and the rejection rate edged down to 12.77%, moving to the 77th percentile from 78th. The spread between spot and contract rates widened slightly to negative $0.23, easing to the 75th percentile from 76th. Against that, the national spot rate rose to $3.56 a mile from $3.54, lifting it to the 97th percentile from 96th, while rail volume held in the 99th percentile and van contract\n& Market Monitor Exclusive\nKeep reading with or Market Monitor\nFull access to this article is reserved for\nand Market Monitor customers.\nExisting customers, log in\nLog in to Log in to Market Monitor\nNot a customer yet? Learn more about both platforms →",
